  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 445 PM CDT Mon Aug 10 2026\n\nOnce morning fog burned off we quickly transitioned to another\nwarm/hot and steamy afternoon. Mid to even upper 70s dew points\nare common across south central Nebraska, which when combined\nwith air temperatures in the upper 80s and lower 90s is still\nleading to heat indices around or just above 100 degrees.\nFurther south into KS, drier air has worked in from the SW, but\nthis is just helping to raise air temperatures even further into\nthe upper 90s to around 100 degrees. So no matter where one is\nin the CWA this afternoon, it certainly still feels like summer.\n\nMain question this evening surrounds the ability for convection\nto maintain itself as it rolls E off the High Plains,\npotentially into our far W zones as early as 02Z-03Z. Most of\nthe recent CAM guidance weakens this activity pretty quickly\nafter sunset thanks to rapidly increasing ML CINH. However, its\npossible that a strengthening low level jet (up to 35-45kt)\ncould help offset or delay some of this weakening. IF storms can\nmaintain some intensity, the main threat would be gusty winds of\n50-60 MPH, and would likely remain mainly W through NW of the\nTri-Cities. Activity should weaken to mainly just lingering\nshowers by around and esp. after midnight. Unfortunately, this\ndoesn't appear to be a big rain-maker, even for the areas that\ndo get hit, owing to the expected weakening trend.\n\nAnother question for the overnight period will be if we see yet\nanother round of fog. Neither deterministic models or ensembles\nhave an overly strong/obvious signature for fog tonight...but\nsimple pattern recognition (light winds/high BL RH) and \npersistence from past few days suggests it's certainly a \npossibility. Guidance that does hint at fog suggests it would be\nmainly N of I-80. Will obviously need to monitor these trends \nthroughout the evening and overnight, but for now, have left out\nof the HWO and just included some fog mention in the grids.\n\nPattern begins to break down on Wednesday as the strong upper\nhigh centered over the Four Corners region now shifts E and\nreorients over the Deep South. This pattern shift will likely\nresult in more active zonal or even SW flow aloft, with a parade\nof weak disturbances supporting frequent rounds of convection\nthat initially develops along the high plains during the\nafternoon, then shifts E during the evening/night. Moisture will\nlikely NOT be a limiting factor as a SE CONUS upper ridge in\nAugust often allows for monsoonal moisture to be pulled well N \nthen E onto the Plains to interact with the frequent upper \ndisturbances. There will likely be at least SOME severe weather \nand heavy rain potential in this type of pattern nearly daily \nthrough the weekend, and this begins with the entire forecast \narea being in either a marginal or slight risk (level 1-2 of 5) \non Wednesday.\n\nWith the more frequent rain chances will also come increased \ncloud cover and gradually cooler temperatures. We probably \nwon't be able to shake the humidity (still have a lot of \nevapotranspiration happening each day), but the upper pattern \ndescribed above should shunt any significant low level thermal \nridging further S, allowing for mainly 70s and 80s for Friday \nand into the weekend during any daytime dry breaks.",
